  {
    "key": "dogAge.scienceSection.title",
    "value": "Why we don't use the \"7× rule\""
  },
  {
    "key": "dogAge.scienceSection.body",
    "value": "The popular \"multiply by 7\" formula has no scientific basis. Dogs mature rapidly in the first 2 years (reaching sexual maturity and full size), then age more slowly thereafter. Our calculator uses size-adjusted life stage guidelines from: the American Animal Hospital Association (AAHA) and the 2020 UCSD epigenetics methylation study (Labrador reference). Larger breeds age faster due to higher metabolic rates and faster cell division — not because of size itself."
  },
